/// A sequence of integers, constructed with range(start, end) or range(start,
/// end, step): the values start, start+step, ..., staying on the end side of
/// step — for the two-argument form (step 1) start, start+1, ..., end, both
/// bounds included, an end below start being empty. The range literal is its
/// surface syntax: a..b is the closed range, a...b the half-open one (the larger
/// end excluded), iterated from the a side toward the b side, so 9..0 is the
/// descending range(9, 0, -1). It is constructed by the two- or three-argument
/// conversion form the way error("msg") is by the one-argument form — a native
/// constructor that builds the value — and is not a stored collection: its
/// bounds and step are kept lazily, so a wide range costs nothing until walked.
///
/// range impls foldable<nint, nint>, so it iterates with for and carries the whole
/// provided method set (count, any, all, map, filter, keys, values) for free.
/// Its key is the 0-based position — like a list's index — and its value is the
/// element, so for...of binds the element and for...in binds the position; the
/// K/V semantics are list's exactly.
pub type range = builtin impl comparable {
  /// The == operator: two ranges are equal when their start, end, and step are
  /// equal. A literal and the range(...) it desugars to compare equal, so the
  /// equivalence is checkable directly — (0..9) == range(0, 9).
  pub extern fn eql(other: self): bool
  /// The != operator.
  pub extern fn neq(other: self): bool
} impl foldable<nint, nint> {
  /// The required primitive: visit each element in order with its 0-based
  /// position as the key, threading the accumulator. The provided
  /// count/any/all/map/filter/keys/values are defined on top of this.
  pub extern fn fold(init: A, step: fn(acc: A, key: nint, value: nint): A): A
}
